#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""Admit, sync and revoke host access for people and projects.

Root's 0600 people/<user>.conf records ordered member= lines, locked=true|false,
ssh=<card key> and expires=<card expiry or empty>. No device credential is admitted.
Sync verifies project bundles in an isolated unit and reconciles the verified
persons' project roles, locks, forge teams, Matrix rooms and allowed signers.
Project locks leave other memberships, sessions and tokens intact. Host locks,
current-key revocation and last-membership removal revoke the whole account.

registry runs for root or sudo -n from the unlocked, unrevoked host admin pinned in
root's 0600 registry/pin.conf, which root alone, never through sudo, writes once at
setup with registry pin <user> <key>.
It copies the admin's plain private setup/registry.bundle as sync does, or with
--stdin, root's alone, takes the bundle root gives before any account exists; it verifies it
through porelo registry verify --json against the pinned key's fingerprint and the
applied commit in the same kind of isolated unit, checks each project and person
alone, naming bad ones while the rest applies, keeps every revoked login revoked
with its last applied entry, refuses an answer naming no host card signer, moves
the pin on a verified handover and records the answer in root's 0600
registry/applied.json. sync refuses a project that answer does not name for its
recorded, unrevoked admin; the host never removes such a project itself.

With account= in forge.conf, admission creates the login and the porelo-<user>
gate and no token, since Forgejo keeps only a token's hash; lock also revokes
each <agent>-<user> a 6j host's agents= names. forge-token, by sudo alone, deletes
every token of the caller's login and answers one new token alone on stdout.
Root can request a named person with forge-token --for <login>, never through sudo;
that token is written to no file. Lock prohibits all those logins and deletes all their tokens
through the forge's private socket API; its temporary admin token stays
in memory. registry applies card keys and locks every person whose current key it revokes.

task <task>, by sudo alone, reads one Ed25519 user certificate of at most 16 KiB
on stdin from a private temporary file with ssh-keygen -L, which checks its
signature. Its signing key must be the caller's key in registry/applied.json, not
revoked; one principal must be task:<task>; it must hold now and end. Then root's
0600 /var/lib/porelo-host/tasks/<user>/<task>.json records it, never replaced,
and the gate's token porelo-task-<task> of porelo-<user> is made anew and answered
alone on stdout. task --end <task> deletes that token; task --show <fingerprint>
answers the record of that task key, read-only, to root or any unlocked person;
sweep, root's alone from its daily timer, deletes every task token whose recorded
certificate has ended. Task and forge-token calls delete their temporary admin
token before reporting success.

Matrix admission stores no token. The gate authenticates the socket's peer.
Lock and purge run the transport's expel <login> as porelo-matrix, including a
purge after an earlier lock; a refusal stops removal. Sync publishes the verified
admins list, one login per line, atomically in matrix/<project>.admins, root's
0640 file in a root:porelo-matrix 0750 directory.

Validation precedes changes; publication of the record is last. Command failures
can leave partial system changes and are reported without their captured output.
Do not manually admit a partial account: inspect and remove its partial access
before retrying. Calls serialize on root's existing forge.conf, without creating
a lock file during validation. Purge removes the record; another purge then
refuses the unknown person with 65, leaving the same state.

The only test hook, _test_entry, exists only when imported under another name.
An installed call runs as __main__ with -I and accepts no path, identity, runner,
or hook overrides from arguments, stdin or the environment.
"""

